    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d398">

                <pb facs="0608" n="538" sameAs="frus:frus1919Parisv01/pg538"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">
                    Breckinridge Long Papers</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">
                        Colonel E. M. House to
                        the Third Assistant Secretary of State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Long</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi>
                        </placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1918-12-15">15 December,
                            1918</date>.<lb/>[Received January 7, 1919.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Long</hi>: I thank you for your letter of the 26th ultimo,
                    enclosing a copy of a memorandum on International Telegraphic Communication,
                    which you handed to Mr. Lansing.<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d398-f1"/> I shall read this memorandum with great interest,
                    as the subject is one I am very much concerned about, and I trust that something
                    can be worked out at the Conference along the lines that you have indicated.</p>
                <p>With very best wishes, I am,</p>
                <p>Faithfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">E. M. House</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
